it was the syrian president's first  visit to an arab country since an  uprising against his rule began in 2011  but the uae's engagement with the once  widely shunned bashar al-assad is not  new and it has been happening despite  opposition from long-time ally the  united states the biden administration  was swift in condemning the visit saying  it is profoundly disappointed and  troubled by an attempt to legitimize a  leader responsible for atrocities but  many believe this was not just about  bringing damascus back to the arab fold  by hosting assad the uae wanted to make  a statement to the west  look you guys have left us as in the  middle east in a vacuum  by your partial withdrawal  the partnership that we have with the  united states is no longer functioning  you're not delivering on the security uh  on the security guarantees that you're  giving particular as we're looking  towards the jcpoa 2.0 being negotiated  with iran and the uh saying we're taking  over responsibility we you want to share  the burden we are taking the burden but  we're doing it our way  russia's invasion of ukraine has exposed  rifts between washington and its allies  in the middle east the uae and saudi  arabia the gulf nations which host u.s  forces have so far avoided taking a  position against russia it's not just  that they are not supporting u.s  president joe biden's administration as  it tries to cut moscow's lifelines  the uae and saudi arabia are two of the  world's biggest oil exporters  uk prime minister boris johnson was in  riyadh earlier this month to try to  convince the west's long-time allies to  increase production to rein in soaring  energy prices and help end the west's  dependency on russian oil  the saudi  emirati alliance is not so eager  to  support united states and western europe  and europe in general  attempt to stop the russia and ukraine  by refusing to produce and to increase  oil production and to lower gas prices  which making it costly for the united